The smash product is enumerated by pairing neighbourhoods, collapsing a factor that is the master, and its combinators are computable.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`smashEnum`).** A paired index enumerates the product neighbourhood of its two components when both are proper; otherwise it enumerates the smash master neighbourhood.

**Theorem (`smashEnum_master`).** If at least one indexed component is the master neighbourhood, the smash enumeration returns the smash master.

*Proof.* The proper-pair condition fails in the defining alternative. ∎

**Theorem (`smashEnum_proper`).** If both indexed components are proper, the smash enumeration returns their product neighbourhood.

*Proof.* The proper-pair condition holds in the defining alternative. ∎

**Theorem (`smashEnum_eq_eff`).** Given Boolean characteristic functions for proper component neighbourhoods, the smash enumeration is the product of the selected component indices, where a failed properness test selects the appropriate master index.

*Proof.* If both tests succeed, both original indices are selected; if either fails, both selections yield master neighbourhoods and hence the smash master. ∎

**Theorem (`smashEnum_eq_prodNbhd_mem`).** Every enumerated smash neighbourhood is a product neighbourhood of two component neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* Use the indexed components in the proper case and the two masters otherwise. ∎

**Theorem (`smashEnum_mem`).** Every set in the smash enumeration is a smash neighbourhood.

*Proof.* A proper pair gives a proper smash neighbourhood, while every improper pair gives the master. ∎

**Theorem (`smashEnum_subset_master`).** Every enumerated smash neighbourhood is included in the product of the component masters.

*Proof.* Every neighbourhood is included in the smash master, which is that product. ∎

**Theorem (`proper₀_dec`).** Properness of a numbered neighbourhood in the first component is recursively decidable.

*Proof.* Equality with the numbered master is decidable, so its negation is decidable. ∎

**Theorem (`proper₁_dec`).** Properness of a numbered neighbourhood in the second component is recursively decidable.

*Proof.* Equality with the numbered master is decidable, so its negation is decidable. ∎

**Definition (`smashPresentation`).** The smash Scott presentation uses the smash enumeration and decides intersection equality and consistency by reducing them to the corresponding component relations and properness tests.

**Theorem (`smashPresentation_X`).** The neighbourhood numbered by an index in the smash presentation is its smash-enumerated neighbourhood.

*Proof.* This is the presentation's defining enumeration. ∎

**Definition (`smashProj₀`).** The first smash projection relates \(W\) to \(X\) when both are neighbourhoods and the left preimage of \(W\) is included in \(X\).

**Theorem (`smashProj₀_rel`).** The relation of the first smash projection is exactly the stated neighbourhood and left-preimage inclusion condition.

*Proof.* This is its defining relation. ∎

**Definition (`smashProj₁`).** The second smash projection relates \(W\) to \(Y\) when both are neighbourhoods and the right preimage of \(W\) is included in \(Y\).

**Theorem (`smashProj₁_rel`).** The relation of the second smash projection is exactly the stated neighbourhood and right-preimage inclusion condition.

*Proof.* This is its defining relation. ∎

**Definition (`smashPaired`).** Pairing two approximable maps relates a source neighbourhood to the smash master automatically, and to a proper product neighbourhood when the two maps relate it to the respective factors.

**Theorem (`smashPaired_rel`).** The relation of smash pairing is precisely the disjunction between the master case and the proper componentwise case.

*Proof.* This is its defining relation. ∎

**Definition (`smashMap`).** Two approximable maps act componentwise on smash products, with every source related to the target master and proper products related through the two component maps.

**Theorem (`smashMap_isComputable`).** The componentwise smash map of two computable maps is computable.

*Proof.* Proper target pairs reduce the relation to the two recursively enumerable component relations after primitive-recursive index selection; an improper target is always related. ∎

**Theorem (`smashPaired_isComputable`).** Smash pairing of two computable maps is computable.

*Proof.* For a proper target pair the relation is the conjunction of the two component relations; for the target master it holds automatically. ∎

**Theorem (`smashProj₀_isComputable`).** The first smash projection is computable.

*Proof.* On enumerated neighbourhoods its relation is decidable inclusion of the effectively selected left component into the target neighbourhood. ∎

**Theorem (`smashProj₁_isComputable`).** The second smash projection is computable.

*Proof.* On enumerated neighbourhoods its relation is decidable inclusion of the effectively selected right component into the target neighbourhood. ∎

**Theorem (`smash_isEffectivelyGivenS`).** The smash product of two effectively given systems is effectively given in the Scott sense.

*Proof.* Choose presentations of both factors and use their smash presentation. ∎
